// Module `float`: f32 and f64 (SEM-2). CORE operations (mul_add, to_i64 and its siblings,
// total_order) are core.md's and not repeated. `T` ranges over the float types. Nothing
// here allocates.
//
// N4 in SPEC-GAPS: SEM-2 says conversions from integers to floats round to nearest even,
// but core.md names no operation for them and has no from_bits. Library code could only
// assemble the Eisel-Lemire mantissa from float literals bit by bit and scale it by
// powers of two, which is slow and double-rounds subnormals. `parse` needs a core
// `float.from_int[T, U]` (correctly rounded) or `float.from_bits`.

/// NEEDS CORE (N4). Parses the whole of s as a decimal float: optional '-', digits with an optional
/// fraction, an optional exponent (JSON number syntax; "inf" and "nan" are refused).
/// Correctly rounded (round-to-nearest-even). A value too large for T is Ok(infinity);
/// callers that need a finite number test `float.is_finite`.
/// Fails: Error(Empty | Invalid(i)), i the byte position; never OutOfRange.
/// Alloc: nothing; the slow path keeps at most 768 significant digits in a fixed-size
/// local record, as Rust's dec2flt does. Cost: O(len); Eisel-Lemire fast path,
/// big-decimal slow path linear in the digits. Ex: `let x: f64 = float.parse(token)?`
pub fn parse[T](s: String) -> Result[T, ParseError]

/// Whether x is neither infinite nor NaN: `x == x` and `x - x == 0.0`. Cost: O(1).
/// Ex: `float.is_finite(x)`
pub fn is_finite[T](x: T) -> bool
